# Mutations and Diffs

This tutorial edits an Agent's routes, members, and Plugins as data with `Jido.Agent.mutate/2`, reads the resulting `Jido.Agent.Diff`, and then returns the same edits from a Turn.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Set up an Agent

The Agent starts with one route. The other modules are the parts the mutations add.

```elixir
defmodule MutationGuide.Refund do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "mutation_guide_refund",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{amount_cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{amount_cents: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | refunded_cents: state.refunded_cents + amount}}
  end
end

defmodule MutationGuide.FraudReviewer do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "fraud_reviewer"

  routes do
    route "refund.review" do
      action _input, schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state}
      end
    end
  end
end

defmodule MutationGuide.RefundLog do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts), do: {:refund_log, Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)}
end

defmodule MutationGuide.RefundDesk do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "refund_desk"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{refunded_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/billing/refunds"

    route "refund.issue", MutationGuide.Refund, as: :issue, defaults: %{amount_cents: 100}
  end
end

desk = MutationGuide.RefundDesk.instantiate!(id: "desk-1")
Enum.map(desk.routes, & &1.path)
```

The cell returns `["refund.issue"]`.

## Apply a mutation

`Jido.Agent.mutate/2` takes an Agent and an ordered list of mutation Directives. It returns a validated candidate and the Diff. The source does not change. No process is involved.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

{:ok, candidate, diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [
    Directive.route_add("refund.partial", MutationGuide.Refund, as: :partial),
    Directive.route_update("refund.issue", %{defaults: %{amount_cents: 500}})
  ])

{
  Enum.map(desk.routes, & &1.path),
  Enum.map(candidate.routes, & &1.path),
  Enum.map(diff.routes.added, & &1.path),
  Enum.map(diff.routes.updated, & &1.after.defaults)
}
```

The cell returns:

```text
{["refund.issue"], ["refund.issue", "refund.partial"], ["refund.partial"], [%{amount_cents: 500}]}
```

Instances and definitions both mutate. A definition has no state, so Plugin state edits do not apply to it.

## Read a Diff

A `Jido.Agent.Diff` describes the final difference between the two values.

| Field | Content |
| --- | --- |
| `fields` | Changed top-level fields, as `%{field => %{before: _, after: _}}` |
| `state` | `nil`, or `%{before: _, after: _}` |
| `routes`, `agents`, `connections`, `plugins` | `%{added: [], removed: [], updated: [], order: %{before: [], after: []}}` |

Jido matches routes by path, members by name, connections by `{from, to, path}`, and Plugins by provider. Each `updated` entry is `%{before: _, after: _}`.

`Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?/2` checks one field, and `Jido.Agent.Diff.empty?/1` checks them all. A reorder counts as a change. An edit that a later edit cancels does not.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

{:ok, _same, cancelled} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [
    Directive.route_add("refund.partial", MutationGuide.Refund),
    Directive.route_remove("refund.partial")
  ])

{Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?(diff, :routes), Jido.Agent.Diff.changed?(diff, :plugins),
 Jido.Agent.Diff.empty?(cancelled)}
```

The cell returns `{true, false, true}`.

## Know what fails

A batch is atomic. One failure returns an error, with the index of the failing operation in `path`, and no candidate.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

{:error, duplicate} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [
    Directive.route_add("refund.partial", MutationGuide.Refund),
    Directive.route_add("refund.partial", MutationGuide.Refund)
  ])

{:error, missing} = Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [Directive.route_remove("refund.void")])

{:error, order} = Jido.Agent.mutate(candidate, [Directive.routes_reorder(["refund.partial"])])

{:error, not_a_mutation} = Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [Directive.server_stop()])

Enum.map([duplicate, missing, order, not_a_mutation], &{&1.code, &1.path})
```

The cell returns:

```text
[
  already_exists: [:directives, 1],
  not_found: [:directives, 0],
  invalid_route_order: [:directives, 0],
  invalid_mutation: [:directives, 0]
]
```

Jido checks references only in the final candidate. The order of a batch can therefore add a forward route before the member it targets. A batch that leaves a route pointing at nothing fails.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

forward = %{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :agent, name: :fraud}}

{:ok, with_member, member_diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [
    Directive.route_add("refund.review", forward),
    Directive.agent_add(:fraud, MutationGuide.FraudReviewer)
  ])

{:error, dangling} = Jido.Agent.mutate(with_member, [Directive.agent_remove(:fraud)])

{Enum.map(member_diff.agents.added, & &1.name), dangling.code}
```

The cell returns `{[:fraud], :unknown_forward_target}`.

## Add and remove a Plugin

A Plugin edit also manages the Plugin's state field on an instance. `plugin_add/3` initializes it from the Plugin's schema, or from the `:initial` option.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

{:ok, logged, plugin_diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [Directive.plugin_add(MutationGuide.RefundLog, [], initial: 3)])

{logged.state, Enum.map(plugin_diff.plugins.added, & &1.provider), plugin_diff.state.before}
```

The cell returns `{%{refunded_cents: 0, refund_log: 3}, [MutationGuide.RefundLog], %{refunded_cents: 0}}`.

Removal discards data, so you must say so.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

{:error, %{code: :plugin_state_discard_required}} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(logged, [Directive.plugin_remove(MutationGuide.RefundLog)])

{:ok, removed, _diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(logged, [Directive.plugin_remove(MutationGuide.RefundLog, state: :discard)])

removed.state
```

The cell returns `%{refunded_cents: 0}`.

`Jido.Agent.Directive.plugin_configure/3` changes a Plugin's options. When the options change the state key, it needs a `:migrate` option of `{module, function, args}`. That function receives the old value first and returns `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, reason}`.

## Mutate from a Turn

An Action can return mutation Directives. The Turn applies them to the same candidate as the new state, and the batch still contains them.

```elixir
alias Jido.Agent.Directive

defmodule MutationGuide.OpenRefunds do
  use Jido.Action, name: "mutation_guide_open_refunds"

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(_input, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | refunded_cents: 1},
     [Jido.Agent.Directive.route_add("refund.partial", MutationGuide.Refund)]}
  end
end

{:ok, opener, _diff} =
  Jido.Agent.mutate(desk, [Directive.route_add("refund.open", MutationGuide.OpenRefunds)])

open = Jido.Signal.new!("refund.open", %{}, source: "/billing/admin")
{:ok, opened, [%Directive.RouteAdd{path: "refund.partial"}]} = Jido.Agent.cmd(opener, open)

{opened.state.refunded_cents, Enum.map(opened.routes, & &1.path)}
```

The cell returns `{1, ["refund.issue", "refund.open", "refund.partial"]}`.

An AgentServer computes the Diff for a Turn like this and applies it to the running system. Members start or stop, and changed Plugins reconfigure. See [Change Live Composition](../composition/change-live-composition.md) and [Live Reconfiguration](../plugins/plugin-live-reconfiguration.md).

## Mutation Directives

Every constructor is on `Jido.Agent.Directive`.

| Target | Constructors | Update fields or options |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Routes | `route_add/3`, `route_replace/3` | `:as`, `:defaults`, `:state`, `:reply` |
| Routes | `route_update/2` | `:handler`, `:as`, `:defaults`, `:state`, `:reply` |
| Routes | `route_remove/1`, `routes_reorder/1`, `routes_set/1` | Reorder needs every current path |
| Members | `agent_add/3`, `agent_replace/3` | `:count`, `:initial_state` |
| Members | `agent_update/2` | `:definition`, `:count`, `:initial_state` |
| Members | `agent_remove/1` | none |
| Connections | `connection_add/2`, `connection_replace/2` | `:from`, `:to`, `:delivery` |
| Connections | `connection_update/2` | `:from`, `:to`, `:changes`, where changes holds `:delivery` |
| Connections | `connection_remove/2` | `:from`, `:to` |
| Topology | `topology_set/1` | Replaces all members and connections |
| Plugins | `plugin_add/3` | `:initial` |
| Plugins | `plugin_configure/3` | `:migrate` |
| Plugins | `plugin_remove/2` | `:state`, which accepts `:discard` |

A route handler can be an Action or Flow module, or a handler Map such as `%{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :parent}}`.

